import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
	title="Why Bicycle Theft Matters | Bait Bikes"
	description="Bicycles are transportation, work tools, public-service equipment, and high-value property. Learn why bicycle theft deserves a serious response."
	kicker="Why bikes"
>
	<header class="page-hero shell">
		<p class="eyebrow">Mobility is practical infrastructure</p>
		<h1>Everyone rides. Every ride connects to something.</h1>
		<p class="lede">A stolen bike can mean a missed shift, appointment, class, delivery, family obligation, emergency response, or chance to participate in public life. Better bicycle protection is both crime prevention and mobility policy.</p>
	</header>

	<section class="section light">
		<div class="shell">
			<div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Bikes are tools</p><h2>A bicycle is often the way the rest of the day works.</h2></div><p>The impact is not captured by replacement value alone. Time, access, health, work, insurance deductibles, specialty fit, adaptive equipment, child transport, and trust in public space are all part of the loss.</p></div>
			<div class="card-grid">
				<article class="card"><span class="number">COMMUTE</span><h3>Work and care</h3><p>Health workers, hospitality staff, tradespeople, public employees, caregivers, and shift workers use bikes when routes or schedules make other transportation impractical.</p></article>
				<article class="card"><span class="number">SERVICE</span><h3>Public institutions</h3><p>Police, fire, EMS outreach, park staff, campus teams, postal and delivery workers, and community organizations use bicycles to reach people and places.</p></article>
				<article class="card"><span class="number">ACCESS</span><h3>Family and independence</h3><p>Cargo bikes carry children and goods. Adaptive cycles enable mobility. E-bikes extend range and make trips possible across age, ability, terrain, and weather.</p></article>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="shell split">
			<div class="prose">
				<p class="eyebrow">Value and classification</p>
				<h2>“Just a bike” can be expensive property crime.</h2>
				<p>High-end road, mountain, cargo, adaptive, and electric bicycles can cost thousands of dollars. Depending on the property value, conduct, and local law, a theft may cross a felony or “grand theft” threshold. Thresholds and offense names differ, so agencies and public materials should describe the applicable law precisely rather than generalize.</p>
				<p>Federal prosecutions also demonstrate that stolen bicycles can be transported across state or national borders as part of resale schemes. That does not mean every theft is organized, but it does mean report quality and cross-jurisdiction connections matter.</p>
			</div>
			<aside class="aside-panel"><p class="eyebrow">Protect the owner record</p><ul><li>Record the serial and component identifiers</li><li>Keep purchase and service documentation</li><li>Photograph the complete bike and unique marks</li><li>Use a high-quality lock and secure anchor</li><li>Report theft promptly with complete facts</li><li>Do not personally confront a suspected thief</li></ul></aside>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="shell callout">
			<p class="eyebrow">Our point of view</p>
			<p class="quote">We are bicycle chauvinists in the most useful sense: we believe bikes deserve infrastructure, product engineering, reporting systems, investigative attention, and public policy equal to the role they already play.</p>
			<cite>Protecting bicycles is not a niche cause. It is protection for the people and communities who depend on them.</cite>
			<div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/partners/">Build a community partnership</a><a class="button secondary" href="/operations/">Plan responsible operations</a></div>
		</div>
	</section>
</SiteLayout>
